; every line in an engineering drawing has a specific meaning.
; the type of line (weight, style, pattern) IS the language.
; a viewer trained in engineering drawing reads the lines the way
; a musician reads notation — each symbol carries precise meaning.
; 
; VISIBLE OUTLINE (continuous thick, 0.5-0.7mm):
; the edges and outlines you can see from that view.
; the most important line — defines the shape.
; 
; HIDDEN DETAIL (dashed thin, 0.25-0.35mm):
; edges and features hidden behind visible surfaces.
; a hole going through the back — shown as dashes.
; allows seeing internal features without section views.
; 
; CENTRE LINE (chain: long dash, dot, long dash, 0.25mm):
; axes of symmetry. centres of circles, holes, cylinders.
; extends slightly beyond the feature. always used for circles.
; two intersecting centre lines mark the centre of a circle.
; 
; CUTTING PLANE (thick chain with arrows at ends):
; shows where the section cut is taken.
; arrows show direction of viewing for the section.
; labelled A-A, B-B to match the section view.
; 
; DIMENSION LINE (thin continuous with arrowheads):
; shows the extent of a measured dimension.
; arrowheads touch the extension lines.
; value written above the line (ISO) or in a gap (ASME).
; 
; EXTENSION LINE (thin continuous):
; extends from the feature to the dimension line.
; small gap at the feature. extends slightly beyond dimension line.
; 
; HATCHING (thin continuous at 45°):
; fills cut surfaces in section views.
; shows material has been cut.
; different patterns for different materials (steel, cast iron, aluminium).
; 
; PHANTOM LINE (chain: long dash, two dots):
; shows alternative positions of moving parts.
; shows adjacent parts (context, not part of this drawing).
; shows repeated features.

(kosha line-types
  (vishesa drawing-varga engineering-varga)
  (swarupa subanta)
  (yukta visible-line hidden-line centre-line dimension)
  (abheda language))
